## [ENT-036] | 2026-11-16 03:36 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/plans/hauler.json | see: ENT-030
Class: a figure measured for one set of pilots taken for another (the family of ENT-015 and ENT-030: a figure acted on without the check that binds it).
Case, 2026-11-16 02:56 to 03:27 UTC: `fill.py fill hauler --write`, run by me, put mission-cracking-the-shell-central into the hauler plan as paying work on its measured 1,410 cr; that figure came from a pilot of another role.
The record: 12 hauler runs, every one `cracking_the_shell not offered`: 8 at -26 cr, 4 at 0 cr, -208 cr in all (data/routes/runs.jsonl). The board does not offer the mission to haulers; why is not found.
Effect: -208 cr and 12 short runs; no ship hit, no hold touched.
Cause: I let the filler's list stand without opening the record the figure rested on, and the dispatcher hands a once route to every free pilot in turn, so one wrong figure costs one run per pilot.
Fix, done: both Cracking the Shell routes out of the hauler plan at 03:27 UTC (commits 123c4cc, b0626b8); my practice: a once route added to the hauler plan is watched until its first hauler RESULT line, and dropped at the first `not offered` (LEARNINGS.md, unpromoted).
Fix, proposed (Unity's): fill.py counts a once route as open to a role only after one pilot of that role was offered it (reports/2026-11-16_apollo_harvester-made-by-hand-pays-only-from-own-silicon.md, Suggestions).
Would it have prevented this one: the proposed fix, yes; my practice, only from the second run on.

## [ENT-037] | 2026-11-16 03:59 UTC | Unity | error | ref: communications/queue.log | see: ENT-031
A mechanism I had read in a help text and never run went into a script, a job prompt, a skill, my memory, a notice to the fleet and a word to Suggi. Class: a claim written as fact without its test (the family of ENT-031, case 3).
- What happened. At 02:52 UTC I resumed the five turn jobs; two whose stored next-run time lay in the past started a turn at once (02:53). To keep the scheduled slots of 04:00 to 05:00 UTC from starting a second turn beside the round by hand, my script paused the jobs and was to put each back with `cron resume --at <08:00 to 09:00>` when its run ended. I had read `--at` in the command's help. I wrote in queue.log ENT-060 and told Suggi that the next round is 08:00 to 09:00 UTC.
- What the record shows. Each of the five calls was refused: "Cannot re-arm recurring jobs: re-arm is one-shot-only". The script's fallback then resumed each job plainly, with its next run at 04:00, 04:15, 04:30, 04:45 and 05:00 UTC. I found it at 03:56 UTC in the script's own lines, four minutes before the first slot.
- What it cost. The scheduled round of 04:00 UTC follows the round of 03:02 directly: five more sessions within the hour, and the Admirals start it with six requests to me unanswered (ENT-083, ENT-084, ENT-089 to ENT-092). I left the schedule as it stands: Suggi's word is that the jobs stay on, and no tested way makes a recurring job skip one slot.
- Cause. An option in a help text is not a mechanism until it ran once. The fallback made it worse: it reached the state the script checked (`scheduled`) and printed no word of the refusal.
- Fix. The wrong line is corrected in the prompt of my turn job, my skill collective-agents and its turn-job reference, my memory and BOX.md. The rules there now: a mechanism is run once on one job and read back from the job file before it is written anywhere; a round by hand is not started within ninety minutes before the Admirals' slots; a script prints every refusal it met. Open: a tested way to skip one slot (`--catch-up off`, `--grace-minutes`), not built.

