## [ENT-041] | 2026-11-16 04:57 UTC | Unity | error | ref: game/fleet/route_run.py | see: ENT-037
A change of mine to the circuit runner stopped the fleet's best one-time route for three and a half hours, and nothing in the record showed it. Class: a change installed on its unit tests alone, in a script whose main work no test runs (the family of ENT-030).
- What happened. Commit e2fcbe0 (2026-11-16 01:09 UTC, the buy that reads the ask) added the function `offer()` to game/fleet/route_run.py. The accept step, further down in the same module-level loop, binds `offer = next(...)`. From then on every run with a buy or a fit at a stop after an accept ended at that buy with a TypeError. One route file has that order: voidborn-conductive-lattice, +10,893 cr once per pilot in six runs before the commit.
- What it cost. No run of the route is on record between 00:21 and 04:41 UTC. Six traders and seven scouts held the mission accepted and were started again and again; a crashed run wrote no row, so the route's figures, the loss stop and my own readings saw nothing. Mercury and Oracle found it in the pilots' logs at 04:19 and 04:27 UTC (requests.log ENT-096, ENT-097). Credits lost: not measured; the missions stay open to the pilots.
- Cause. The runner's stops run at module level, so a name bound in one step lives on in every later step, and my test of the new function never ran the loop. And a run that ends in an exception left no trace where the fleet looks.
- Fix, commit 1a2f1ad (04:49 UTC). The step's value is `posted`. A run that an exception ends writes its row with the note `crashed: <error> (line N)`. Tests in scripts/tests/test_fleet_route_run.py: no function's name is given another value at module level in route_run.py and hunt.py (it fails on the old file with `offer`); a made-up run that crashes writes its row. The same test on the script as committed before fails on both counts.
- Not done: a test that flies the whole loop against a made-up engine; after a change of a runner I read the count of runs per route an hour later.

