Cause: the host's stop raises SystemExit in the runner at its next sleep, before mine.py's own end (DELETE of the loop, the sale, the row); queue.log ENT-132 says the run "ends and writes its row". I used the stop for the first time without reading what a mine run does on it.
Fix: mine, done: my skill intel-turn names the pitfall (a host stop of a mine run leaves its loop and writes no row; a plan change takes effect when the run ends). Asked: the runner's end runs on a host stop (requests.log, the entry after this one).
Would it have prevented this one: the skill line yes, by not stopping; the runner fix yes, by itself.

