Cause: the host's stop is a signal to the runner, and mine.py stops the engine loop only on its normal way out; my script waited for the dock without asking why the goal was refused.
Fix, done: catch.py of my skill logistics-stock stops a loop left behind (DELETE /accounts/<pilot>/loop) after a minute of 409 "A loop is running", for a pilot held by our own hand with no runner; both loops were stopped so at 04:01 UTC and the delivery finished at 04:11 UTC. Asked, not built: mine.py stops its loop in a finally, so a host stop ends it too (requests.log, this turn).
Would it have prevented this one: the catch fix yes (the loop is stopped after a minute); the mine.py change yes, by itself.

